fw:Obs_ContainerScoped a backlog:MetricObservation ;
    rdfs:label "Container scoping, and what still spans lineages"@en ;
    backlog:observesMetric fw:Metric_Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:observationFor fw:Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:hasObservedValue 0.0 ;
    backlog:observedAt "2026-08-27T19:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasObservationMethod "Thirty-three containers scoped by reading what their members and references belong to, not by naming them: 31 lineage-scoped, 2 framework-scoped. Four were missed on the first pass because they are not subclasses of WorkItemContainer and the membership walk started there — the walk was right and its starting point too narrow. Roadmap spans six lineages and Scope three; both are genuinely cross-lineage and stay live. Residual live-to-archive edges: 14, from two containers, down from 7 edge types across 5." .

fw:Inv_PerLineageContainer backlog:hasInvariantStatus backlog:Holds ;
    backlog:hasRationale "Holds with a stated exception. Every container now declares its scope, and 31 of 33 serve exactly one lineage and archive with it. Roadmap and Scope span lineages by their nature — ranking and boundary-setting across runs is what they do — so they stay live and retain named pointers into the archive. A pointer into a named archive file is a reference, not a dangling edge, which is why archiveFile is required of an archived lineage." .

#################################################################
#  v9.0.0 — six lineages set down.
#
#  807 individuals and 7071 triples moved to
#  backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl, which the ordinary
#  validation path does not load. That is the point: finished work
#  was being re-checked by 299 SPARQL constraints on every release.
#
#  The Lineage individuals STAY LIVE. They are the index, and an
#  index that archives with its contents cannot be searched. Each
#  now names the file its contents went to, so a reader can find
#  what was set aside rather than discover that it vanished.
#################################################################



#  Scope, Roadmap, Commitment_Dev and DoD moved to the archive
#  after the split, on evidence rather than on the earlier rule.
#
#  I had scoped them FRAMEWORK-WIDE because they span several
#  lineages. Measured after the move: Scope holds 14 references and
#  ZERO to live work, Roadmap 12 and zero, the other two none at
#  all. Spanning several lineages does not make a container
#  framework-wide when every one of those lineages is retired — it
#  makes it a retired container with a wide reach.
#
#  The rule is corrected: a container is framework-scoped when it
#  serves the LIVE lineage, not when it once served many.

#  The live register needs its own commitment. Commitment_Dev went
#  to the archive with the lineage it was made for, and nothing
#  replaced it — the clause caught that a backlog with no committed
#  goal is a list. Written now rather than left, because the live
#  lineage HAS a goal and simply never recorded the commitment to
#  it separately from the objectives underneath.
fw:Commitment_Ontology a backlog:GoalCommitment ;
    backlog:commitsToGoal fw:Goal_GovernanceInOntology ;
    backlog:hasCommitmentRationale "The register commits to the mission-facing goal of the ontology-driven lineage: that the governance model is expressed as ontology rather than as documentation. Recorded at v1.133.0 when the archive split removed Commitment_Dev and the clause reported a backlog with no committed goal." .
fw:Register backlog:hasCommitment fw:Commitment_Ontology .

#################################################################
#  v9.1.0 — the archive flags said both true and false.
#
#  Found by reading the PUBLISHED files rather than trusting last
#  release's report. Every Lineage carried lineageArchived false
#  from its declaration AND true from the block appended when the
#  archive was written. Both on a FUNCTIONAL property, and no
#  clause noticed: owl:FunctionalProperty is a statement OWL
#  reasoners enforce and SHACL does not, and this suite is SHACL.
#
#  Consequence: the six archived lineages reported themselves as
#  not archived to any query that took the first value. The archive
#  file was real, the split was real, and the register said it had
#  not happened.
#
#  Third time this session a functional property has carried two
#  values — hasInvariantStatus twice, and priorVersion once. The
#  pattern is always an append that should have been a replace.
#################################################################

fw:Obs_FinalState a backlog:MetricObservation ;
    rdfs:label "State at the close of the ontology-driven lineage"@en ;
    backlog:observesMetric fw:Metric_Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:observationFor fw:Obj_CodeDecisions ;
    backlog:hasObservedValue 0.0 ;
    backlog:observedAt "2026-08-27T21:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasObservationMethod "Read from the published files, not from the previous release's report — which is how the archive-flag defect was found. Four shipped checkers PASS: adoption, criterion resolution, number origin, self-application. Clause proof reports 112 of 302 unproven, up from 92 because this lineage added clauses faster than it added fixtures. Three invariants stand Violated by design: Inv_StoryDecomposition, Inv_ClauseProven and Inv_ArtefactNotProperty, each recording a gap that is real and not yet closed." .

#################################################################
#  v9.2.0 — Inv_ClauseProven worked down, and a naming defect
#  caught along the way that made the first measurement lie.
#
#  A fixture proving 25 targeted clauses was written, and the
#  clause-proof checker filters fixtures by FILENAME — it only
#  scans files containing "negative", "adversarial" or
#  "digestfail". The fixture was named fixture_sparse_shapes and
#  the checker never read it: proven fixtures were shown firing by
#  direct invocation while the tool's own count stayed at 112,
#  because the tool was silently ignoring the very file meant to
#  move it.
#
#  Caught by comparing a TRUE baseline (fixture entirely removed)
#  against the measured after-state, rather than trusting a single
#  number — the two were identical, which is the sign a comparison
#  is broken rather than a sign nothing moved.
#
#  Renamed to fixture_sparse_shapes_negative and re-measured:
#################################################################

fw:Ev_ClauseProofReduction a backlog:TestEvidence ;
    backlog:hasTestSpec "03-tooling/fixtures/fixture_sparse_shapes_negative_v1_0_0.ttl" ;
    backlog:hasTestId "Inv_ClauseProven-reduction" ;
    backlog:evidenceVerified true ;
    backlog:verifiedAt "2026-08-27T22:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:hasVerificationMethod "One fixture, one case per unproven sh:property clause on lightly-used classes: Budget, WorkItemContainer, DeploymentUnit, AdaptationGate, KickOff, Lineage, Milestone, Mission, PlanBaseline, FitGapFinding, ModelKind, DimensionalCost, Forecast, PriorityScore. Measured with a true before-state (the fixture entirely absent, not merely incomplete) against a true after-state: 112 to 80. The first attempt to measure this reported no change because the checker filters fixtures by filename and the file was not named to match — a defect that made the comparison lie by testing itself against itself." ;
    backlog:verifiedByTool "backlog_clause_proof" ;
    backlog:attestsCriterion fw:AC_EP_ScriptDecisions_S1 .

fw:Inv_ClauseProven backlog:hasRationale "80 of 302 unproven, down from 112. 32 clauses closed by one fixture covering the sparse-shape population. Remaining are mostly cross-item structural rules (decomposesInto pointing at real items, dependsOn cycles) needing multi-node fixtures rather than single bare individuals, which is a larger and separate piece of work." .

